<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<!-- Merged into the bundle's generated Info.plist by Tauri's macOS bundler.

     Both keys are load-bearing, not boilerplate, and the audio harness
     (tools/audio-capture-harness) measured why:

     - Without NSMicrophoneUsageDescription, the first microphone access
       kills the process outright - macOS aborts an app that captures without
       declaring why.
     - Without NSAudioCaptureUsageDescription, a CoreAudio process tap for
       far-end audio does not fail: it succeeds and returns silence, at
       real-time pacing, with no error to catch. Measured on macOS 26.5 as
       281 callbacks with peak amplitude 0.0000 while audio was playing.

     The strings are the exact text macOS shows in the consent dialog, so they
     say what Skia actually does with the audio rather than something vague
     enough to be read as evasive. -->
<dict>
    <key>NSMicrophoneUsageDescription</key>
    <string>Skia listens to your microphone during meetings to transcribe your side of the conversation. Audio stays on this device unless you configure a cloud transcription provider.</string>
    <key>NSAudioCaptureUsageDescription</key>
    <string>Skia captures the audio of your calls (the other participants' side) to transcribe meetings. Audio stays on this device unless you configure a cloud transcription provider.</string>
</dict>
</plist>
